# Abstract

This paper proposes a hybrid deep learning framework for decoding imagined speech from EEG signals. Instead of directly processing raw EEG signals, the authors first convert EEG recordings into sequences of topographic brain maps. Three hybrid architectures—3DCNN-LSTM, 3DCNN-StackLSTM, and 3DCNN-BiLSTM—are then used to learn both spatial and temporal EEG representations. The proposed models are evaluated on multiple imagined speech classification tasks, demonstrating improved performance over previous approaches. 

---

# Research Objective

The primary goal of this research is to improve imagined speech decoding by combining:

- Spatial feature extraction using 3D CNN
- Temporal sequence learning using recurrent neural networks
- EEG topographic representations instead of raw EEG signals

The authors also compare different LSTM variants to determine which architecture performs best for imagined speech classification. 

---

# Problem Statement

EEG-based imagined speech decoding remains challenging because:

- EEG signals have a low signal-to-noise ratio.
- EEG is highly nonlinear.
- Brain activity varies across individuals.
- Traditional machine learning struggles to capture complex spatial-temporal relationships.

This work attempts to overcome these limitations using hybrid deep learning models capable of learning richer EEG representations

---

# Dataset

The experiments use the **BCI Competition 2020 Imagined Speech Dataset**.

Dataset characteristics:

- 15 participants
- Frontal EEG electrodes
- Five imagined words
- Short and long imagined phrases
- Publicly available dataset

The paper also compares its performance with KaraOne and Pressel datasets from previous studies. 

